From 7804319eaf1bb391fac09473e1938fd444184189 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:19:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: Hardware clock is Automatic by default, and never
 writes the clock

LibreELEC Hades reads the clock of the computer (the RTC) and never writes
it, taking it to keep local time, as Windows keeps it. A computer whose
clock keeps UTC (one without Windows) then started with its time off by
the time zone until the time came from the internet, unless "Hardware
clock" was set to UTC by hand, which also has the clock written, as
LibreELEC does.

"Hardware clock" gets a third choice, Automatic, the default where
nothing was chosen: once the time has come from the internet, the system
measures what the clock keeps (clock-measure keeps its verdict in
/storage/.cache/clock-measured), and from the next start the init reads
it so, a clock that keeps UTC as UTC without writing it (it makes
/run/hades/rtc-never-written for that). Local time and UTC set by hand
stay as they were, and Automatic never overrides them: local time is now
written as a file that says LOCAL, so that it is told apart from nothing
chosen. A choice of local time made before this version wrote no file,
and is Automatic now: on a computer whose clock keeps local time that
reads it as local time, as before.

The list shows the verdict on Automatic ("Automatic: UTC"), and "from the
next start" while that is not what holds now. The help says what
Automatic does, in English and the three Spanish translations, and the
explanation shown before UTC is set says that Automatic reads such a
clock without writing it.

At the start of the service the clock is not written (hwclock --systohc)
where the init reads it as UTC without writing it, as for a clock in
local time.
